 ## Rules
-- What a service publishes or answers is declared in `contracts/`, and nothing else leaves it: the schemas are strict. Why: the contract is all another service is allowed to know (ADR 0001). Enforced by: `services/orders/test_contract.py` and `services/billing/test_contract.py`, with `tools/schema.py`. [2024-06]
-- A contract change is additive: a field is added, nothing is renamed, nothing is removed, no type is changed. Why: `customer_email` renamed to `email` on 12 May 2026, 1,840 order confirmations never sent (`docs/postmortems/2026-05-renamed-field.md`). Enforced by: `services/orders/test_contract.py`. [2026-05]
+- What a service publishes or answers is declared in `contracts/`, and nothing else leaves it: the schemas are strict. Why: the contract is all another service is allowed to know (ADR 0001). Enforced by: `services/orders/test_contract.py` and `services/billing/test_contract.py`, with `tools/schema.py`. The repository already breaks it: `GET /outbox` of `services/notifications/app.py` has neither a schema in `contracts/api/` nor a `test_contract.py`. [2024-06]
+- A contract change is additive: a field is added, nothing is renamed, nothing is removed, no type is changed. Why: `customer_email` renamed to `email` on 12 May 2026, 1,840 order confirmations never sent (`docs/postmortems/2026-05-renamed-field.md`). (unverified): `test_contract.py` stays green when the schema and the provider change together, and the tests of `billing` and `notifications` make their events by hand; nothing replays a consumer, neither here nor outside the repository. [2026-05]
 - A contract field that another one replaces stays published and gets `"deprecated": true` in its schema; it is not removed. Why: consumers outside this repository read this annotation to plan their migration; agreed with them in September 2026. (unverified) [2026-09]
 - Every contract change is recorded in `contracts/CHANGES.md`, one dated line per contract touched, most recent first. Why: consumers outside this repository follow this file, not our PRs; agreed with them in September 2026, written nowhere else. The file does not exist yet: no contract has changed since. (unverified) [2026-09]
 - An event consumer is idempotent: the broker delivers "at least once", the same event comes back with the same `id`. Why: 212 invoices issued twice on 18 November 2025 (`docs/postmortems/2025-11-duplicate-invoices.md`). Enforced by: `shared/bus.py`, which delivers every event twice in all tests. [2025-11]
-- A service never imports another service's code; what it needs, it reads in the event or asks the API for. Why: `billing` imported the catalog of `orders`, the two could no longer be deployed one without the other (ADR 0001). (unverified) [2024-06]
-- Before opening a PR, run `grep -rnE "^(from|import) services\." services/ shared/ tools/` and review each line by hand: a service imports only itself, `shared/` and `tools/` import no service. Why: it is the only check of the previous rule. [2024-06]
-- An event weighs less than 64 KB, envelope included: no file and no export in an event, publish its address. Why: beyond that, the broker's shared plan cuts the connection with no error on the publisher side; a catalog export lost in October 2024. (unverified) [2024-10]
+- A service never imports another service's code; what it needs, it reads in the event or asks the API for. Why: `billing` imported the catalog of `orders`, the two could no longer be deployed one without the other (ADR 0001). `shared/` and `tools/` import no service. Enforced by: `tools/check_imports.py`, job `boundaries`. [2024-06]
 
 ## Forbidden
-- Pointing `BROKER_URL` at preproduction or production from a workstation or an agent session. Why: a replay launched from a workstation on 9 January 2026 re-sent 3,100 invoice emails to customers (`docs/operations.md`). [2026-01]
